WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.082
Conditional aggregation lets one grouped query calculate several related metrics from the same input rows.

00:00:06.082 --> 00:00:10.967
Suppose we want separate counts for pneumonia and fracture admissions for each patient.

00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:16.827
We could run separate queries, but conditional aggregation keeps the measures together in one result.

00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:19.567
There are two common patterns in this module.

00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:25.492
CASE is widely portable and can convert a condition into a value that an aggregate can summarize.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:33.442
PostgreSQL FILTER attaches a condition directly to an aggregate and is often easier to read when each measure

00:00:33.442 --> 00:00:33.832
has its own condition.

00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:39.557
The important idea is that the group stays the same while different subsets of rows contribute to different

00:00:39.557 --> 00:00:44.537
measures. Conditional aggregation lets one grouped query calculate several targeted measures.

00:00:44.537 --> 00:00:48.992
For example, a department row can show total admissions, emergency admissions,

00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:51.437
and elective admissions side by side.

00:00:51.437 --> 00:00:55.902
FILTER is concise in PostgreSQL; CASE is widely portable.

00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:58.847
Both require you to define the condition precisely.
